    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d671">

                <pb facs="1030" n="1012" sameAs="frus:frus1947v03/pg1012"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24557H/2–447</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of War</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Patterson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">and the Secretary of the Navy</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Forrestal</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-02-04">4 February
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: There is inclosed a
                        statement<anchor n="1" xml:id="d671-f1"/> of the views of the Joint Chiefs
                    of Staff on the military implication of a change in the status, under the Treaty
                    of Paris of 1920, of the Spitzbergen Archipelago.</p>
                <p>We wish to reinforce these views by stating that in our opinion it would
                    seriously impair the overall security interests of the United States to accede
                    to any substantial Soviet demands with respect to Bear Island and the
                    Spitzbergen Archipelago. Even in open negotiation among all non-enemy powers
                    signatory to the Treaty of 1920, we <pb facs="1031" n="1013" xml:id="pg1013"/>
                    would recommend that the United States oppose any Soviet gains which could be
                    interpreted in this country or abroad as appeasement of the U.S.S.R.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Forrestal</hi>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of the Navy</hi>
                    <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert</hi> P. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Patterson</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of War</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
